    content: `California has one of the most detailed and strictly enforced mechanic's lien statutes in the United States. Governed by California Civil Code sections 8000 through 9566, the state's construction lien law protects contractors, subcontractors, material suppliers, and laborers who contribute to the improvement of real property. This guide covers everything you need to know about filing a mechanic's lien in California in 2026.

## Who Can File a Mechanic's Lien in California?

California grants lien rights to a broad range of construction participants. Direct contractors (those who contract directly with the property owner), subcontractors, material suppliers, equipment rental companies, laborers, and even architects and engineers who contribute to the improvement of real property all have the right to file a mechanic's lien.

The key requirement is that the claimant must have contributed to a "work of improvement" on real property. This includes new construction, remodeling, renovation, repair, demolition, grading, and landscaping. It also includes furnishing materials, equipment, or supplies that are used or consumed in the work of improvement.

## The Preliminary Notice Requirement

California's preliminary notice requirement is one of the strictest in the country. Under Civil Code section 8200, any claimant other than a direct contractor must serve a Preliminary Notice within 20 days of first furnishing labor, services, equipment, or materials to the project.

The notice must be served on the property owner, the direct contractor, and the construction lender (if any). The notice must contain specific information including the claimant's name and address, a description of the labor, services, equipment, or materials being furnished, the name and address of the person who contracted for the work, and a description of the job site sufficient for identification.

The 20-day deadline is measured from the date the claimant first furnishes labor, services, equipment, or materials to the project. If the notice is served late, the claimant's lien rights are limited to labor, services, equipment, or materials furnished within 20 days prior to the date the notice was actually served, plus any amount furnished after the notice.

Direct contractors are exempt from the preliminary notice requirement but are still subject to all other lien requirements.

## Filing Deadlines

California imposes strict deadlines for recording a mechanic's lien that depend on whether a Notice of Completion or Notice of Cessation has been filed by the property owner.

If no Notice of Completion or Cessation is filed, all claimants have 90 days from the completion of the work of improvement to record their mechanic's lien. "Completion" is defined as the date of actual completion of the work of improvement, occupancy or use by the owner, or cessation of labor for a continuous period of 60 days.

If a Notice of Completion is filed by the property owner, direct contractors have 60 days from the date of recording the Notice of Completion to file their lien. All other claimants have 30 days from the date of recording the Notice of Completion.

These shortened deadlines make it critical for subcontractors and suppliers to monitor whether a Notice of Completion has been filed.

## Recording the Mechanic's Lien

The mechanic's lien must be recorded with the County Recorder's Office in the county where the property is located. California requires the lien to contain a statement of the claimant's demand after deducting all just credits and offsets, the name of the owner or reputed owner, a general statement of the kind of work furnished by the claimant, the name of the person who hired the claimant, a description of the property sufficient for identification, the claimant's signature verified under penalty of perjury, and proof of service of the preliminary notice (if required).

## Service After Recording

Within 10 days of recording the mechanic's lien, the claimant must serve a copy of the recorded lien on the property owner. Service may be made by personal delivery, certified mail, or registered mail.

## Enforcement

After recording the lien, the claimant must file a lawsuit to enforce the lien within 90 days. This 90-day period cannot be extended unless the property owner agrees to a credit or payment plan. If no lawsuit is filed within the 90-day enforcement period, the lien is automatically released and becomes unenforceable.

The enforcement lawsuit is a foreclosure action, similar to a mortgage foreclosure. If successful, the court may order the property sold to satisfy the lien claim, along with attorney's fees, costs, and interest.

## Lien Release Requirements

If the claimant receives payment, they must file a lien release with the County Recorder's Office. California imposes penalties on claimants who fail to release a lien after receiving full payment, including potential liability for the property owner's attorney's fees and damages.

## Special Considerations for California

California uses a system of conditional and unconditional lien waivers that must be exchanged in connection with progress payments and final payments. Using the correct waiver form is essential, as California law prescribes specific statutory waiver forms that cannot be modified.

California also provides special protections for owner-occupied residences and includes a "Stop Payment Notice" remedy that allows claimants to intercept funds held by a construction lender. This remedy is particularly valuable on projects financed by construction loans.

## Conclusion

California's mechanic's lien law is powerful but demanding. The 20-day preliminary notice deadline, the variable lien recording deadlines, and the 90-day enforcement period leave little room for error. Contractors working in California should make lien compliance a standard part of their business operations and consider using professional assistance to ensure all requirements are met.`,
