    date: "2026-04-22",
    content: `Texas has one of the most complex mechanic's lien statutes in the United States. Governed by Texas Property Code Chapter 53, the state's lien law features unique calendar-based deadlines, different requirements for residential and commercial projects, and distinct rules for original contractors versus subcontractors and suppliers. This guide provides a thorough overview of Texas mechanic's lien law as it applies in 2026.

## Who Can File a Mechanic's Lien in Texas?

Texas grants lien rights to original contractors (those who contract directly with the property owner), subcontractors, sub-subcontractors, material suppliers, and laborers. Equipment rental companies also have lien rights for equipment that is used in the construction or repair of improvements to real property.

The key distinction in Texas is between "original contractors" and everyone else. Original contractors have a direct contractual relationship with the property owner and are subject to different notice and deadline requirements than subcontractors and suppliers.

## Notice Requirements

Texas has a distinctive notice system that uses calendar-based deadlines rather than the day-count deadlines used in most other states.

Original contractors are not required to send a preliminary notice before filing a lien. However, they must send a written notice of the unpaid balance to the property owner on or before the 15th day of the third month after the original contractor's work was last performed or materials were last delivered.

Subcontractors and suppliers have stricter notice requirements. They must send a preliminary notice to the property owner and the original contractor by the 15th day of the second month after the subcontractor or supplier first furnished labor or materials. For example, if a subcontractor first performs work on March 5, the preliminary notice must be sent by May 15.

Additionally, subcontractors and suppliers must send a notice of the unpaid balance to the property owner and original contractor by the 15th day of the third month after the month in which the labor or materials were last provided.

For residential homestead projects, there are additional requirements. The original contractor must provide the owner with a list of all subcontractors and suppliers before work begins or within a reasonable time after they are engaged.

## Lien Filing Deadlines

Texas uses the same calendar-based system for lien filing deadlines. Original contractors must file their lien affidavit by the 15th day of the fourth month after the month in which the original contractor's work was last performed or materials were last delivered.

Subcontractors and suppliers must file by the 15th day of the third month after the month in which the subcontractor or supplier last provided labor or materials. Note that the subcontractor's deadline is one month shorter than the original contractor's deadline.

## Filing the Lien Affidavit

In Texas, the lien document is called a "lien affidavit" and must be filed with the County Clerk in the county where the property is located. The affidavit must be sworn under oath and must contain the amount of the claim, the name and last known address of the property owner, a general statement of the kind of work done or materials furnished, the name and address of the person who hired the claimant, a description of the property sufficient for identification, and the dates when the work was first and last performed.

Within five days of filing the lien affidavit, the claimant must send a copy to the property owner by certified mail to the owner's last known address.

## Constitutional Protections for Homesteads

Texas provides special constitutional protections for residential homesteads. Under Article XVI, Section 50 of the Texas Constitution, a mechanic's lien on a homestead is only valid if the work was contracted for by both spouses (if the property is community property) and the contract was signed before work began.

This constitutional requirement means that an original contractor who begins work on a homestead without a properly signed contract cannot enforce a mechanic's lien, even if the work was clearly authorized. This is one of the most important and unique features of Texas lien law.

## Enforcement

A mechanic's lien in Texas must be enforced by filing a lawsuit within one year (for residential homestead projects, two years) from the last day the claimant could have filed the lien affidavit. This deadline is longer than in many other states, giving claimants more time to pursue litigation.

## Lien Waivers

Texas has a statutory lien waiver form that went into effect in 2022. The statute provides four types of waivers: conditional waiver and release on progress payment, unconditional waiver and release on progress payment, conditional waiver and release on final payment, and unconditional waiver and release on final payment.

Using the correct statutory form is important because non-conforming waivers may not be enforceable.

## Practical Tips for Texas Contractors

Texas's calendar-based deadline system creates an unusual situation where your deadline depends heavily on what day of the month you first performed work. Starting work on the last day of a month versus the first day of the next month can shift your deadlines by an entire month.

Additionally, the distinction between residential homestead projects and commercial or non-homestead residential projects is critical. The constitutional protections for homesteads add layers of complexity that do not exist on commercial projects. When working on a homestead, always ensure the contract is properly executed by all parties with an interest in the property before beginning work.

Finally, Texas's notice requirements are not optional. Subcontractors and suppliers who fail to send timely notices lose their lien rights entirely. Given the calendar-based system, it is essential to track the month of first furnishing and calculate all subsequent deadlines from that reference point.`,
