    date: "2026-04-30",
    content: `When you are not paid for construction work, your two primary legal remedies are a mechanic's lien and a payment bond claim. These remedies serve the same basic purpose, ensuring that contractors, subcontractors, and suppliers are paid for their contributions to a construction project, but they operate in fundamentally different ways and apply to different types of projects. Understanding the distinction between them is essential for any construction professional.

## What Is a Mechanic's Lien?

A mechanic's lien is a legal claim placed directly against the real property where work was performed. When you file a lien, you are asserting a security interest in the property itself, similar to a mortgage. The lien encumbers the property title, making it difficult for the owner to sell or refinance until the lien is resolved. If the lien is not paid, you can file a lawsuit to foreclose, which could result in the property being sold to satisfy your claim.

Mechanic's liens are available on private construction projects in all 50 states. They protect the full range of construction participants, including general contractors, subcontractors, material suppliers, and laborers.

## What Is a Payment Bond Claim?

A payment bond is a type of surety bond that guarantees payment to subcontractors, suppliers, and laborers on a construction project. Unlike a mechanic's lien, a payment bond claim is not directed against the property itself. Instead, it is a claim against the surety company that issued the bond.

Payment bonds are required on most public construction projects under the federal Miller Act and analogous state "Little Miller Acts." Because you cannot file a mechanic's lien against government-owned property, the payment bond serves as a substitute remedy.

On private projects, payment bonds are not always required, but many property owners and lenders require them as a condition of the construction contract.

## Key Differences

The most fundamental difference is what each remedy is directed against. A mechanic's lien is a claim against the property. A payment bond claim is a claim against the surety company.

This has practical implications. When you file a mechanic's lien, the property owner has a strong incentive to resolve the claim because the lien clouds their title. When you make a payment bond claim, the property is not at risk. The surety company is responsible for paying the claim.

Another important difference is the scope of coverage. Mechanic's liens are generally available to anyone who contributes to the improvement of real property. Payment bond claims may be limited to certain tiers of the construction chain. Under the federal Miller Act, only first-tier and second-tier subcontractors and suppliers can make bond claims.

## When to File a Mechanic's Lien

You should file a mechanic's lien whenever you have not been paid for work on a private construction project and you have complied with all prerequisite requirements. The mechanic's lien is typically your strongest remedy because of the leverage it creates through the encumbrance on the property.

You should not attempt to file a mechanic's lien on a public project. Government property is immune from liens, and a purported lien against public property is invalid.

## When to Make a Payment Bond Claim

You must make a payment bond claim when the project is a public (government) project, since mechanic's liens are not available. You may also make a payment bond claim on a private project where a payment bond has been provided.

Payment bond claims have their own notice requirements and deadlines, which are separate from the mechanic's lien requirements. Under the federal Miller Act, a claimant who does not have a direct contract with the general contractor must give written notice to the general contractor within 90 days of last furnishing labor or materials. The bond claim lawsuit must be filed between 90 days and 1 year after last furnishing.

## Can You Pursue Both?

On private projects where a payment bond exists, you may be able to pursue both a mechanic's lien and a payment bond claim simultaneously. This dual approach maximizes your leverage. However, most states do not allow double recovery.

The ability to pursue both remedies simultaneously gives you negotiating leverage and protects you in case one remedy proves unavailable or insufficient. For example, if your mechanic's lien turns out to be defective due to a procedural error, you may still recover under the payment bond claim.

## Practical Considerations

When deciding which remedy to pursue, consider the type of project (public vs. private), the amount of the claim, your position in the construction chain, and the timing of your claim.

The type of project often determines which remedy is available. On public projects, the bond claim is your only option. On private projects without a bond, the mechanic's lien is your only option.

Your position in the construction chain affects your options. First-tier subcontractors and suppliers typically have the broadest rights under both mechanic's lien law and payment bond law.

The timing of your claim is critical for both remedies. Missing a notice or filing deadline for either remedy can permanently eliminate that option. Track all deadlines carefully and take action well before any deadline expires.

## Conclusion

Mechanic's liens and payment bond claims are complementary tools in the construction payment toolkit. Understanding when each remedy applies, the procedural requirements for each, and how they interact allows you to protect your payment rights comprehensively. On every project, identify whether lien rights, bond rights, or both are available, and take the necessary steps to preserve all available remedies.`,
