    date: "2026-05-05",
    content: `Subcontractors face unique challenges when it comes to getting paid on construction projects. Unlike general contractors, who have a direct contractual relationship with the property owner, subcontractors are one or more steps removed from the money source. This means that even when the property owner has paid the general contractor in full, the subcontractor may not see payment if the general contractor diverts funds, goes bankrupt, or simply refuses to pay. Mechanic's lien rights exist specifically to protect subcontractors in these situations, but only if those rights are properly preserved.

## Understanding Your Position in the Payment Chain

As a subcontractor, you are typically hired by the general contractor, not by the property owner. This means you have no direct contractual relationship with the entity that ultimately controls the project's funding. Your contract is with the general contractor, and your right to payment depends on the general contractor's willingness and ability to pay.

This structural vulnerability is precisely why mechanic's lien rights are so important for subcontractors. A mechanic's lien allows you to assert a claim directly against the property, bypassing the general contractor and reaching the property owner's most valuable asset.

However, because you lack a direct relationship with the property owner, most states impose additional requirements on subcontractors before they can file a lien. These typically include preliminary notice requirements that inform the owner of your involvement in the project. Meeting these requirements is not optional.

## Best Practice 1: Send Preliminary Notices Immediately

The single most important step any subcontractor can take to protect their lien rights is to send the required preliminary notice on the first day of work. Do not wait to see if there will be a payment problem. By the time a payment dispute becomes apparent, the preliminary notice deadline may have already passed.

In California, you have 20 days. In Florida, 45 days. In Texas, until the 15th of the second month after first furnishing. In Michigan, 20 days. In Ohio, 21 days. In every state that requires a preliminary notice, the clock starts running the moment you first furnish labor or materials.

Make preliminary notice filing a standard part of your project onboarding process.

## Best Practice 2: Track Every Deadline on a Calendar

Mechanic's lien law involves multiple overlapping deadlines, and missing any one of them can eliminate your rights. At minimum, you need to track the preliminary notice deadline, the lien filing deadline, the lien service deadline, and the enforcement deadline.

Use a calendar system with advance reminders. Set alerts for 30 days, 14 days, and 7 days before each deadline. Do not rely on memory or informal tracking methods.

## Best Practice 3: Document Everything from Day One

Strong documentation is the foundation of a successful mechanic's lien claim. If you ever need to enforce a lien through litigation, you will need to prove when you first and last furnished labor or materials, what work you performed, the value of your work, that you complied with all notice requirements, and that the amount you claim is accurate.

Start documenting on your first day at the project. Keep daily logs, photograph your work at regular intervals, and save all contracts, change orders, purchase orders, invoices, delivery tickets, and correspondence.

## Best Practice 4: Know Your State's Rules

Mechanic's lien law is entirely state-specific. The rules in one state may be completely different from the rules in a neighboring state. If you work in multiple states, you must learn the requirements for each jurisdiction.

Key questions to answer for each state include whether a preliminary notice is required, what the lien filing deadline is, what information must be included in the lien document, who must be served with a copy of the recorded lien, and whether there are special rules for residential versus commercial projects.

## Best Practice 5: Do Not Sign Unconditional Lien Waivers Before Being Paid

Lien waivers come in two forms: conditional and unconditional. A conditional waiver becomes effective only when the payment specified in the waiver is actually received. An unconditional waiver is effective immediately upon signing, regardless of whether payment has been received.

Never sign an unconditional lien waiver until you have received and deposited the payment and confirmed that the funds have cleared. Signing an unconditional waiver before being paid permanently releases your lien rights, even if the check bounces.

## Best Practice 6: Act Quickly When Payment Problems Arise

When you notice the first sign of a payment problem, act immediately. Do not wait to see if the situation resolves itself. Payment problems in construction rarely improve on their own.

Your first step should be to communicate directly with the general contractor in writing. If the general contractor does not respond, consider sending a Notice of Intent to File a Mechanic's Lien to both the general contractor and the property owner.

If the dispute is not resolved and your filing deadline is approaching, file the lien. Do not wait until the last day.

## Best Practice 7: Maintain Professional Relationships

Filing a mechanic's lien or even sending a preliminary notice can create tension in professional relationships. However, experienced construction professionals understand that these are standard business practices, not personal attacks.

Frame your notices and lien filings as routine business procedures that you follow on every project. Most general contractors and property owners who are familiar with the construction industry will not take offense.

## Conclusion

Protecting your lien rights as a subcontractor requires proactive planning, consistent processes, and timely action. The contractors who get paid are not necessarily the ones who do the best work. They are the ones who understand the lien system, comply with its requirements, and act decisively when payment problems arise. By following these best practices on every project, you can ensure that your lien rights remain intact and that you have the legal tools necessary to collect what you are owed.`,
