      "Recognize the early warning signs of construction payment problems before they become critical. Learn what to watch for and how to protect yourself.",
    date: "2026-05-10",
    content: `Payment problems in the construction industry rarely appear without warning. In most cases, there are identifiable warning signs that precede a payment default by weeks or even months. Recognizing these signs early gives you time to take protective action, such as sending notices, adjusting your work, or filing a mechanic's lien, before a payment dispute escalates into a crisis. This article identifies seven warning signs that experienced contractors watch for on every project.

## Warning Sign 1: Payments Are Consistently Late

The most obvious warning sign is when payments start arriving late. In the construction industry, delayed payments are unfortunately common, and many contractors accept slow payment as a cost of doing business. However, there is an important distinction between occasionally slow payments and a pattern of increasingly late payments.

When payments that were arriving 30 days after invoice submission start taking 45 days, then 60 days, then 75 days, you are seeing a trend that frequently precedes a default.

The danger of accepting slow payments is that each delay moves you further past your lien filing deadlines. If you continue working while payments are becoming slower and slower, you may find yourself in a position where your lien rights have expired by the time the payment problem becomes a full default.

When you notice a pattern of increasingly late payments, communicate in writing with the party who owes you payment, verify that you have sent all required preliminary notices and that your lien filing deadlines have not passed, and consider slowing or stopping work until payment catches up.

## Warning Sign 2: The General Contractor Asks You to Delay Billing

If the general contractor asks you to hold your invoices, delay your billing, or resubmit invoices in a different format for reasons that seem vague or inconsistent, pay close attention. Requests to delay invoicing are often a sign that the general contractor is managing a cash flow problem.

A general contractor who is having trouble collecting from the owner, or who has financial problems of their own, may ask subcontractors to delay billing to manage the timing of their obligations. This creates a dangerous situation for the subcontractor because delayed billing can push you past your lien deadlines without your realizing it.

## Warning Sign 3: Change Orders Are Not Being Processed

On most construction projects, change orders are a normal part of business. When change orders stop being processed, or when the general contractor begins disputing change orders that were previously agreed upon verbally, it often signals a financial problem.

Be especially cautious if you are being asked to continue performing changed work without a written, signed change order. Performing work without documentation makes it much harder to collect payment later.

## Warning Sign 4: Subcontractor Turnover on the Project

If you notice that other subcontractors on the project are leaving, being replaced, or filing liens, take this as a serious warning sign. Subcontractor turnover is often the result of payment problems, and if other subs are not being paid, there is a significant risk that you will face the same problem.

Talk to other subcontractors on the project. You may also check public records to see if other mechanic's liens have been filed against the property.

## Warning Sign 5: The Property Owner Is Absent or Uninvolved

On well-managed construction projects, the property owner or their representative is actively involved in overseeing progress and approving payments. When the owner becomes absent, unresponsive, or uninvolved, it can be a sign of financial distress, a dispute with the general contractor, or a loss of interest in the project.

An uninvolved owner is problematic for subcontractors because the owner may not be processing payment applications from the general contractor.

## Warning Sign 6: Requests for Lien Waivers in Unusual Patterns

Be alert to any deviation from the standard lien waiver pattern. Red flags include requests for unconditional waivers before payment has been received, requests for waivers that cover amounts larger than the payment being made, requests for final waivers when the project is not yet complete, and pressure to sign waivers quickly without time to review them.

Any of these patterns may indicate that someone in the payment chain is attempting to eliminate your lien rights before you realize there is a payment problem. Never sign a lien waiver under pressure.

## Warning Sign 7: Rumors of Financial Problems

The construction industry is a relationship-driven business, and information travels quickly through professional networks. If you hear rumors that the general contractor, the property owner, or another key party on the project is in financial trouble, take these rumors seriously.

While rumors should not be treated as facts, they should prompt you to verify the situation through independent research. Check public court records for lawsuits, review the company's bonding status if possible, and monitor the project more closely for other warning signs.

## What to Do When You Spot Warning Signs

When you identify one or more of these warning signs, take the following steps in order of priority. First, verify your notice compliance. Confirm that all required preliminary notices have been sent and that your lien filing deadline has not passed. Second, document the warning signs with written notes including dates and specific facts. Third, communicate in writing with the general contractor and property owner. Fourth, consult with a construction attorney if the signs are serious. Fifth, consider filing a mechanic's lien if payment is overdue and the warning signs suggest the problem will not resolve on its own.

The key principle is to act early. Every day you wait while warning signs accumulate is a day closer to your lien deadline. The contractors who protect themselves most effectively are those who respond to the first warning sign, not those who wait until the problem becomes a crisis.`,
